ply  within the modeling team but Peter was  the figurehead and so Peter was the the  guy that sort of carried the the bulk of  this  so what happened overall well this is  the amazing thing Omicron arrived on  campus four days after it was it was uh  discovered four days and we were hoping  for spring semester but it was just  shortly after this thing was discovered  there's no way our testing regimes were  able to keep up with its transmissivity  and so we had a large number of  infections 2 800. almost entirely  actually undergraduates but there were  some graduate students as well  no hospitalizations so uh consistent  with other things you will have heard  about Omicron  and the big thing that I want to  highlight is that this focus on  real-time data really did change the way  that Cornell responded to the situation  so then we had to sort of say well now  that we've sort of somehow managed to  hobble through to the finish line on the  fall semester what do we do next so our  data suggested from contact tracing data  that Omicron was three to five times  more transmissible than Delta  um  so students are kind of done with them  asking you know they were kind of fed up  at that point we all were right and so  we just concluded that very large  student infection counts were just going  to be part of the deal for the spring  and so we had models that that we're  looking at if we test once a week twice  a week and so on and you see that these  curves are bending down but the  infection counts are very large here so  we were concerned that there wasn't much  we could do  so the good news is that the as as we've  seen the serious health effects are  unlikely most of the cases are mild but  I do recognize that this group is is  sort of expert in Long covered it's an  area I know nothing about so that was  something that we did not factor into  our calculations  so another thing that we had seen and  this I also want to highlight infected  students almost never infect uh  non-student employees that staff and  faculty  um so  um we didn't see that kind of  transmission moving uh into the staff  and faculty which meant that the  infections tended to be confined to them  for the most part amongst the younger  population  so  um we were now operating in a new world  where the case counts were going to be  large and as I said earlier  um we were previously our models were  sort of previously designed to be sort  of more accurate at the lower case  counts but when we got to these larger  case counts  um maybe then the differential equation  kind of uh style modeling is more  appropriate still with uncertainty  quantification on the top so still with  parameter uncertainty that you have to  think through that that was the new  style of modeling that we looked at and  one thing that was very interesting that  came out of that modeling was that  testing is only  useful in that it reduces quarantine and  isolation  uh if you do it enough if you don't do  it enough then you don't significantly  um cut these infections and there's also  a problem with that if you if you're  testing at a certain rate you are  detecting cases by law we're required to  isolate those cases so there's a kind of  a there's a catch-22 here test enough  you're catching lots of potentially mild  cases that you would otherwise not worry  too much about but that when you catch  those cases you have to quarantine and  isolate so even the asymptomatic cases  right that you wouldn't otherwise find  testing may find those so there was this  very difficult question of how much  should we test if at all going forward  so  one other critical thing was we we we  found that you know we had to predict  like what would happen um as the the  spring semester progressed a big issue  is that when you think about those case  counts that you're seeing in the early  stages of some outbreak  the people that are most likely to be  infected are the more social individuals  those with more