efully  about the parameters for would have been  helpful that would have been great  um but really it was the parameter  uncertainty that was the The Big Driver  of all of these decisions and the  modeling part was was it was it was work  but it wasn't the the core  so the uncertainty quantification how do  you represent your uncertainty about  certain key input parameters and there  are many of them right so you have to be  thinking about uh the sort of  relationships between those parameters  um the second Point uh early infections  are more likely to be the more social  individuals and the issue that's going  on here is if you just count those count  those cases then you're going to  overestimate what's going on in the  general population and if you're a bit  puzzled by this point think about  something called the Friendship Paradox  which says if you look at how many if  you you know if you think about say  Facebook and you think about how many of  your friends how many friends  do your friends have  so you are an individual look at uh the  set of your friends on Facebook  count the average number of friends they  have  and compare that to the average number  of friends you have it will be larger  the reason it's larger is that you're  friends with somebody who was an  incredible socialite and that person has  an enormous number of friends and so  there's a sort of a systematic way that  this this biases things and so we had to  try to reverse reverse that effect  and finally when you are when you do  have these limited interventions for us  that was testing but it could be  vaccination or many others how do you  extend that to multiple groups in such a  way as to curtail growth rates so we're  working on all of these some of them  statistical some of them uh uh more  optimization based with a group of six  of us that are it's a mix of Public  Health and operations folks and a  sociologists so I will stop there thank  you very much for the chance to be here  Sh