nts who are more social  maybe the ones that are bringing the  virus to to campus and um you know if  they're interacting with each other as  well as other people from outside the  university you know maybe that has a a  factor a bearing on some of the  performance of the models it absolutely  does so  um we we were in close contact with Ed  Kaplan at Yale it is a a real star in  our community who's done just a  tremendous amount of different policy  analysis work including very early work  on needle exchange programs for HIV all  the way through so uh Yale um as I'm  sure you know is a is a sort of a  moderate sized campus but in a very  Urban setting and so  it's a it's a very interesting uh case  in point that compares with with ours  and they were also trying to do regular  asymptomatic screening and uh but they  had many more cases so  um I think it's exactly this point that  you're making that the the contacts with  the local community create a new source  of infections which uh we could we could  you know to a large extent avoid we  still have a local community but nothing  like the size of uh New Haven in  Connecticut  I it was touched by your comment about  uh mbas and St Patrick's Day don't go  well with each other and I was surprised  in looking at some of your your graphs  that uh I didn't it didn't quite connect  with me but I didn't realize that  Cornell was such a party school but  oftentimes the biggest predictor of an  outbreak was whether or not it happened  around you know times of Celebration  let's just say and that I'm curious what  was the University's request of the  student body in an attempt to kind of  avoid those things I mean you know it's  college kids they're going to do their  fraternity parties that sort of thing  but did the University specifically ask  them not to do those things and they did  them anyway or what seemed to be the  sociology there yeah it's a very  interesting  um deal so so the university did have a  behavioral com